Other than teaching people not to waste time and mental effort, and
demonstrating how little the breakable items it may contain actually are
worth?  I think you are rather misunderstanding my point, but that's not
that big a deal, really.

Perhaps my lessons are a bit more sophisticated than they appear at 
first glance.  Perhaps they're designed to help a player who's thinking,
rather than one who's looking to be handheld.  There are a lot of
reasons I phrase my advice the way I do.  Part of that is to encourage
people to ask follow-up questions about what's behind what I say, rather
than the details.

And yes, I do want people to ask follow-up questions.  It shows they're
thinking about what I'm saying and trying to understand why I'd
recommend a strategy that isn't 100% mathematically optimal to save 100%
of all objects.  Janis and ctaboir don't understand this, so I have to
field flak from them about the petty details.  Perhaps others have
learned something different in the meantime.

> them to win, and that they distract you. But just because
> *you* don't need them to win and they distract *you* doesn't
> mean anything for anyone else. 

Of course it does.  It means that it is possible to learn to play well
enough that the precautionary measures are not necessary.  It also means
that I think _everyone_ who is willing to try can learn to play well
enough that the precautionary measures aren't necessary.  And it also
means that I think people will do better learning that as beginners,
rather than after spending a year banging their head against the game.

> Less skilled players (you know,
> the ones who might be reading RGRN for advice) 

(Realistically, almost nobody is reading RGRN for advice.  But that's a
different, past thread.)

> will have a
> better chance at ascending if they open chests in a way that
> risks breaking that orcish dagger they don't use anyway,
> rather than one that risks breaking a potentially useful potion
